  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Just as Nagiyev is about to lose his patience waiting for his aspic, Katya rescues Max by whipping up an instant molecular aspic with horseradish that Nagiyev loves.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Почему? Потому что вы пилоты, да?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Чего я ждать должен свой холодец до Пасхи, а?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Почему? Это чего?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А это как раз тот самый холодец, который я собирался вам принести.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Это его Катя приготовила?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, Дмитрий Владимирович.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Приготовь мне ещё что-нибудь необычное!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Почему?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Потому что мы пилоты!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Спасибо, я твой должник.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Пожалуйста, Макс.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты меня просто спасла.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Почему? Потому что вы пилоты, да?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why? Because you are pilots, right?' Notice how Russian expresses 'why' and 'yes' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Чего я ждать должен свой холодец до Пасхи, а?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why do I have to wait until Easter for my aspic, huh?' Dmitry Nagiyev anchors the line on 'what', while 'and' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Почему? Это чего?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why? What is this?' The grammar packs 'why' and 'what' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А это как раз тот самый холодец, который я собирался вам принести.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And this is that very aspic that I was going to bring you.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'and' before landing on 'to bring'. Dmitry Nagiyev answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Это его Катя приготовила?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Did Katya prepare this?' Notice how Russian expresses 'this' and 'cooked' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, Дмитрий Владимирович.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, Dmitry Vladimirovich.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'yes' and finishing on 'yes'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Приготовь мне ещё что-нибудь необычное!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Prepare something else unusual for me!' An emphatic spoken line centered on the verb meaning 'prepare'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Почему?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why?' Dmitry Nagiyev brings out the contrast between 'why' and 'why' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Потому что мы пилоты!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Because we are pilots!' Max brings out the contrast between 'that is why' and 'what' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Спасибо, я твой должник.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Thank you, I am in your debt.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'thank you' and finishing on 'debtor'. Katya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Пожалуйста, Макс.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You're welcome, Max.' The grammar packs 'please' and 'Max' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты меня просто спасла.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You simply saved me.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'you' before landing on 'to save'. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
